//? Title: Estimate constant runs of ordered coefficients
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Fuse neighboring regression coefficients

The design columns represent consecutive measurement channels. The
fused lasso penalizes changes between adjacent slopes, so neighboring
channels can share a single fitted coefficient. A separate penalty
shrinks the levels toward zero.

The example uses two balanced measurements per channel so its
unpenalized slopes are easy to read. The fitted intercept is common
to all channels. The columns of a general regression design can
contain simultaneous measurements from multiple channels.

```{cpp, id=fused-channel-coefficients, file="figures/fused_lasso_channels.svg", results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/fused_lasso.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge;
    const std::vector<double> raw{.06,.2,.1, 1.3,1.42,1.25, -.7,-.85,-.72};
    const auto channels = raw.size();
    linalg::DenseMatrix<double> design(2 * channels, channels);
    std::vector<double> response(2 * channels), index(channels);
    for (std::size_t j = 0; j < channels; ++j) {
        design(2*j, j) = 1;
        design(2*j + 1, j) = -1;
        response[2*j] = 2 + raw[j];
        response[2*j + 1] = 2 - raw[j];
        index[j] = j + 1;
    }
    const auto fit = stats::fused_lasso(design, response, {
        .lambda_l1 = .07 / channels, .lambda_difference = .28 / channels,
    });
    if (!fit.converged)
        throw std::runtime_error("Expected convergence for the channel coefficients");

    std::println("| First channel | Last channel | Fitted slope |");
    std::println("| ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    std::vector<double> step_x, step_y;
    for (const auto& segment : fit.segments) {
        std::println("| {} | {} | {:.3f} |", segment.begin + 1, segment.end, segment.level);
        step_x.insert(step_x.end(), {segment.begin + .5, segment.end + .5});
        step_y.insert(step_y.end(), {segment.level, segment.level});
    }
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    graphics::RPlot::create()
        .line(step_x, step_y, "Fused fit", {29, 78, 216}, 2.5)
        .points(index, raw, "Unpenalized slopes", {180, 83, 9}, 5)
        .title("Ordered channel coefficients").x_label("Channel").y_label("Slope")
        .y_limits(-1.2, 1.9).size(740, 420).save_svg("build/fused_lasso_channels.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| First channel | Last channel | Fitted slope |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 3 | 0.143 |
| 4 | 6 | 1.067 |
| 7 | 9 | -0.593 |
\image(figures/fused_lasso_channels.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The nine channel slopes are estimated as three constant runs.)
\alttext(Nine unpenalized slopes are grouped in threes. The fitted plateaus are about 0.14, 1.07, and minus 0.59 from left to right.)

The differences penalty leaves two jumps and three constant segments.
The fit reports the segment endpoints in column order.

[Tibshirani et al., Sparsity and Smoothness via the Fused Lasso|https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9868.2005.00490.x]
